shared":15,"play_caller":"Kevin Stefanski"},{"season":2022,"team":"JAX","distance":0.4364,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"CHI","distance":0.4749,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"PHI","distance":0.4656,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"},{"season":2025,"team":"SF","distance":0.6234,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Robert Saleh"},{"season":2023,"team":"CHI","distance":0.6905,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":0.6978,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CHI","distance":0.7199,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Colts went 11-5 in 2020 and outscored opponents 451 to 362. The offense ranked 12th in EPA per play and the defense 9th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 3rd lowest I formation usage and a Cover 6 rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Colts offense was a little above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd lowest I formation usage in the league. Philip Rivers ran the passing game, with T.Y. Hilton leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Colts defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 24th. Justin Houston was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was DeForest Buckner, who had 9.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had a Cover 6 rate that was among the highest in the league.","special":"The Colts were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 50% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 86% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 29% of the money on the books.","players":"Philip Rivers played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,169 yards. Targets were spread around, with T.Y. Hilton and Nyheim Miller-Hines leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Jonathan Taylor and Nyheim Miller-Hines. On defense, DeForest Buckner led the team with 9.5 sacks and Kenny Moore picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Frank Reich called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Jaguars and the 2025 Eagles under Kevin Patullo. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Eagles under Jonathan Gannon and the 2025 49ers under Robert Saleh.","trends":"The offense went from 16th in EPA per play in 2019 to 12th in 2020, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 18th in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 9th in 2020, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was nickel usage, which went from 47% to 77%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}